### Insertion at the Kth Position of a Linked List

**Problem:** Given the head of a singly linked list and two integers `X` and `K`, insert a new node with value `X` at the Kth position of the linked list and return the head of the modified linked list.

**Approach:**

- Create a new node with the given value `X`.
- If `K == 1`, insert the new node at the beginning by setting `newNode.next = head` and return the new node as the updated head.
- Otherwise, initialize a temporary pointer with the head node.
- Traverse the linked list until reaching the `(K-1)th` node.
- Set the next reference of the new node to the next node of the temporary pointer (`newNode.next = temp.next`).
- Connect the previous node to the new node (`temp.next = newNode`).
- The new node is inserted at the Kth position.
- Return the original head.

**Time Complexity:** O(n) – In the worst case, we traverse the linked list to reach the required position.

**Space Complexity:** O(1) – Only one new node and a temporary pointer are used.

**Concepts Used:**
- Singly Linked List
- Kth Position Insertion
- Node Creation
- Head Pointer
- Temporary Pointer
- Pointer Manipulation
- Node Linking
- Reference Handling


###Solution

class Solution {
    public ListNode insertAtKthPosition(ListNode head, int X, int K) {
        // Case 1: Insert at the beginning (1st position)
        if (K == 1) {
            return new ListNode(X, head);
        }
        
        // Traverse to the (K - 1)th node
        ListNode curr = head;
        for (int i = 1; i < K - 1 && curr != null; i++) {
            curr = curr.next;
        }
        
        // Insert the new node after the (K - 1)th node
        if (curr != null) {
            ListNode newNode = new ListNode(X, curr.next);
            curr.next = newNode;
        }
        
        return head;
    }
}
